In chatbot design, managing conversation history effectively is a key pillar for delivering smooth and personalized user experiences. Whether you’re building a simple FAQ bot or a sophisticated AI assistant, your approach to storing and retrieving chat history impacts everything from context continuity to scalability, privacy, and system performance. There’s no one-size-fits-all answer — the right method depends on your architecture, use case, user privacy concerns, and tech stack. In this article, I’ll break down six popular strategies for managing chatbot conversation history, highlighting their pros, cons, and practical tips for implementation. ... The entire conversation history is stored locally on the user’s browser, typically using Local Storage or IndexedDB. Every time the user sends a message, the full conversation history is sent to the server.

